---
name: sdh-release-lifecycle
description: >
  Use when running a release of any SemVer bump — the 11-stage lifecycle
  (request, analysis, spec, spec review, plan, plan review, implementation,
  test, closure, next-release plan, retro), the light-vs-full track choice,
  deciding the bump class at Analysis, and the stage-transition announcement
  rules. The closure checklist itself — skill sdh-release-closure; the
  retrospective — skill sdh-retro.
---

Stages are soft: no strict gating, but **announce every transition** ("moving from stage X to Y"). Trivial fast-lane tasks (`sdh-phase-workflow`) bypass the lifecycle.

## Track

Decided at Analysis.
- **Light** — PATCH, or MINOR with no cross-boundary change and < 3 non-obvious decisions: Request → Analysis → spec-as-chat → Implementation → Test → Closure → Retro. Plan and plan review fold into Analysis.
- **Full** — MAJOR always; MINOR with real design or cross-boundary change (DB schema, contracts, security). All 11 stages.
- Unsure → full.

## Autonomous overlay

- Offer it at spec/mockup acceptance when the rest is mechanical: "I'll run Implementation → Test → the documentation part of Closure without approvals, announcing transitions. OK?" A standing "work without stopping" = granted.
- Runs stages 5–8 and the documentation part of Closure.
- Still stops: (1) the owner's visual sign-off for any visual surface, before Closure; (2) the git block, owner-only, shown once after sign-off. A tool/server whose smoke is automated has no visual gate.
- Stop only at a fork the spec does not cover; answer everything else yourself from the spec, code or a sensible default.
- Leave the mode on a new decision, a blocker, grown scope or a smoke failure; offer it again after.

## Filing tasks

- Every task goes under a `## vX.Y.Z` header — no backlog bucket. Unsure of the exact version → best-guess pre-MAJOR version, or a coarse `## vN.0.0`.
- Renumbering a section: re-read the tasks inside for the old number (e.g. "Close vX.Y.Z").

## A release ships its whole declared composition

- The tasks under the header are the release. The spec starts by naming every one of them; the owner pointing at one task says where to start, not what the release is.
- A task leaves only by the owner's explicit decision, recorded by moving it under another header. Not by silence, low priority or "needs its own spec".
- A gap between spec and section is said out loud at spec review.
- `O-`/`M-` tasks are not part of it.

## Scope grew → re-check the bump class

When a task joins mid-release, re-classify the whole release; the highest-grade change sets the class. Say it in one line; confirm an increase to MINOR/MAJOR with the owner; re-title the header if the number changed. Exception: a task that completes an unmet goal of a shipped release keeps it a PATCH.

## Stages

1. **Request** — the owner's kickoff.
2. **Analysis** — scope, fast-lane vs phase, light vs full, blockers; server surface check (`sdh-api-contract-consume`) for features needing endpoints. **Decide the bump class and version here** and confirm MAJOR/MINOR with the owner. Output: one message with scope, class, version.
   - First check which `## vX.Y.Z` slots are taken in `todo.md` and the roadmap.
   - Measure before calling work large: count with a script or query and name the number.
   - Work touching the DB: read the migrations; a new `CHECK`, `UNIQUE`, `FK` or column makes it at least MINOR.
   - The class follows whether the user gains a new affordance, not the volume of edits.
   - A release completing an unmet goal of a shipped release is PATCH, even with a new setting, migration or field. A class the owner named is closed — never append "by SemVer this is MINOR".
   - The owner picked the riskier option → name one concrete risk; if it is real (data loss, counterparty breakage, user-visible failure) asking again is right. Never argue it a second time.
3. **Spec** — per `sdh-phase-workflow`: context, scope, files to modify, decisions, `## Verification`. Light track: the chat is the spec. Visual surface → mockups first (`sdh-visual-mockups`).
4. **Spec review** — (a) your own explicit pass: ambiguities, contradictions, missing files, mismatched names, i18n, tests — report it, or "no issues"; (b) only real clarification questions; (c) the owner's explicit OK. No plan or code before it.
5. **Plan** *(full)* — tasks in `todo.md` under the confirmed header, each mapped to a decision or verification item.
6. **Plan review** *(full)* — your self-review only; not an owner gate.
7. **Implementation** — task by task per `sdh-phase-workflow` and the project's workflow; each finished task moves to `done.md`.
8. **Test** — the spec's `## Verification` plus automated suites. Test counts reach the Changelog only after the real run. Late bugs: see `sdh-release-closure`.
   - **Scope line first.** A verification list opens with three lines: what it covers, what it deliberately does not, and where to look (branch, version, local or deployed). Name what the owner will see that is already deferred, with its card id.
   - Smoke rows come from the release goals, one per task. Edge paths found in code go to the automated pack.
   - Two tasks touching the same input get one smoke item that runs through both.
   - If the spec names red conditions, the smoke asserts each one, not a weaker substitute.
   - An empty cell, zero or dash on a surface this release built is a defect until the source confirms the emptiness.
   - A smoke item observing a text or effect first shows its carrier: the contract line, or one read of an old object that already has it.
   - A measurement covers what it probed; confirm the path the product cannot start without was among the probes.
   - A probe's failure message prints status and error code, never the raw body (it may carry a secret).
   - A capability shipped switched off gets its live smoke at the flip, as if new.
   - An instrument observing someone else's action (deploy, restart) starts before the action; one started after says which part of the window it missed.
   - A smoke that deliberately fails CI never runs on a commit that will be tagged.
   - Coverage counts from what executes (routes, schema, task list), not from the document being measured.
   - A threshold on a ratio is measured at the scale it protects.
   - A baseline records its configuration (model, limits, prompt, contour); compare only when all still hold.
   - **Visual surfaces: the owner's eyes on the running UI are the smoke.** Closure does not start and the git block is not shown until they approve.
   - Preprod with auth: a gitignored `.env.preprod` holds the credentials to log in for the smoke (not the secrets that boot preprod).
9. **Closure** — `sdh-release-closure`. The tag is the owner's.
10. **Next-release plan** — minutes, not a design session. Re-read the next section: re-estimate from the current code and contract, split what outgrew its estimate, drop what this release made moot, confirm scope with the owner. An estimate older than a month is re-measured; a zero estimate becomes a coarse band with its one-line decomposition. No spec, no decisions here.
11. **Retro** — `sdh-retro`, only after stage 10.

## Transitions

- Every stage that has a skill starts by invoking it fresh (global rules, "How the rules apply").
- Skipping a full-track stage needs the owner's yes; propose it with a one-line reason.
- Loops back are allowed when forced (gap, new constraint, broken premise, grown scope) and announced: "moving from stage 7 back to 3, OK?".

## The gate has no authority over the owner

Criteria, lists and gates bind the agent, not the owner. On "ship it": name the risk once in one line, file the unmet item as a task, go to closure — even if the owner agreed to that criterion earlier. After "I accept", new findings go to the next slot, not into this release.

## Gates

- Repeating a step without progress, or a gate you cannot satisfy: stop, question whether the gate is real or self-made, escalate. The third identical failure means the gate is wrong.
- A gate enforces only the owner's decision, and its text names that decision and whose it is. A gate the owner overrides was wrong: remove it or re-ground it.
